[{:seon.task/name "a-reused-value-is-never-swept-under-its-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps; asked by runtime 2026-11-16 (its :collect now sweeps at quiescence off the writer, and root holds spawns while a sweep runs until this lands). CAUSE [read, both ends]: a commit writes a value of at least :value-out-of-line-bytes out of line under its content key; datahike.index.persistent-set/value-key (persistent_set.cljc ~666-676) writes nothing when the key is cached in this collection generation or the store already holds it (k/exists?), so the key keeps its old konserve :last-write. datahike.gc/gc-storage! marks from the heads, then konserve.gc/sweep! deletes every unreachable key written before its cutoff (konserve gc.cljc:132-137), the cutoff being the gc guard's safe point: the start of the oldest in-flight commit (gc.cljc:160-176). A reused key's write is older than any cutoff, so when the commit that reuses it is in flight at the mark or begins after it, its head lands naming a deleted value (:value-not-found at the next read). The generation (di/collection-key) moves only after the sweep, so the cache trusts the key through the whole sweep. PRIOR WORK: the guard's safe point (datahike.gc-guard), the value cache's generation (jolt/gc-cache-epoch), konserve's sweep cutoff; docs/storage.md#collection names this hazard and Seon's rule that collection runs only while no other process writes the store. OPTIONS: (a) a reuse rewrites the value, so its :last-write moves past the cutoff: one value write a reuse (LMDB writes header, meta and value as one record), across processes, but it spends the bytes the dedup saves; (b) the guard, which already runs where the writers are, spares by key: a commit's reused keys are registered with the guard from its start until its head moves, and while a sweep runs every reuse is kept in the sweep's spared set; konserve's sweep skips a spared key (a :konserve.gc/spare? predicate beside its cutoff). CHOSEN: (b): no write, an in-process set; correct under Seon's rule that a sweep runs only where every writer of the store is (the guard's own model). COST: estimate per reuse one set insert (~100 ticks) while a commit is in flight or a sweep runs, none otherwise; per sweep candidate one set lookup (~50 ticks); memory O(keys reused during one sweep). PROOF, red first: datahike.test.gc-reuse-test/a-value-reused-during-a-sweep-survives-it: a value written, unreachable, then a sweep whose mark is done and a commit reusing the value before the sweep deletes (the sweep's batch-done hook commits): red today (:value-not-found), green with the spared set. Deletes: root's hold on spawns during a sweep (runtime's), once green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.gc-reuse-test/a-value-reused-during-a-sweep-survives-it}
  :seon.task/estimate #:datahike.gc{gc-storage! {:n 1000, :order :linear, :ticks 100000, :bytes 100000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
